so  here we have  the main specimen this is what's left of  the oldest meat eating dinosaur that  once roamed the uk a segment of its  spine part of its pelvis and a thigh  bone are all that scientists had to  piece together its movements the  preservation of this material is  actually so great  that you know we can basically see  everything and we also have the left  femur that belongs to the same  individual and we can actually see how  this femur would have fitted together  with the pelvis so we can actually put  it in the large opening in which it  would have articulated the pendrive  milner might have been related to the  t-rex but it looked very different it  would have been about chicken size  essentially a chicken with a very long  tail at the end so the total length  would have been about a meter long the  animal would have been  you know agile would have been walking  on two legs  would have had sharp serrated teeth  would have had a very active lifestyle  and would have probably been eating  anything uh you know that could have  gotten its hands on that wasn't too big  this animal would have lived more than  200 million years ago so that means that  t-rex was closer to us in time  considerably than it would have been to  pendrick the animal we've described now  all of that detail from these fragments  that went unnoticed for so long  the fossils were found inside this  limestone quarry in camarthenshire in  south wales more than 70 years ago but  were misplaced it was only when  paleontologists were discussing the  triassic period that they rediscovered  the fossils tucked away with crocodile  remains it took them a year to  meticulously analyze those tiny bones  so delicate yet dense with information  about the evolution of the world it's a  piece in the jigsaw puzzle that helps us  to understand the evolution of these  dinosaurs and also it really enriches  our understanding of what the  environment in south wales was like 200  million years ago these c